1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,680
Loopback Transformer:

2
00:00:01,680 --> 00:00:05,870
Towards a Unified View of
Residual Looped Transformers.

3
00:00:06,880 --> 00:00:09,530
Linear attention is a memory over tokens.

4
00:00:10,140 --> 00:00:14,050
Each token writes its key and value
into a state that is passed along,

5
00:00:14,180 --> 00:00:16,050
and later tokens read from it.

6
00:00:16,380 --> 00:00:18,850
The residual stream does
the same over depth.

7
00:00:19,240 --> 00:00:21,770
Each block writes its
update into the stream,

8
00:00:21,940 --> 00:00:25,030
which carries it forward to the
blocks that read from it later.

9
00:00:25,440 --> 00:00:26,070
Unrolled,

10
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:28,540
every read is a weighted
sum of what was written,

11
00:00:28,540 --> 00:00:32,030
and the standard residual
fixes every weight at one.

12
00:00:33,140 --> 00:00:35,590
Every residual rule fits one interface.

13
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:37,650
It carries a memory forward,

14
00:00:37,940 --> 00:00:40,040
writes each block's result into it,

15
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:41,970
and reads the next input from it.

16
00:00:42,280 --> 00:00:46,570
The standard residual keeps one stream
and adds every update with weight one.

17
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:50,310
Hyper-Connections keep several
streams and learn how to mix,

18
00:00:50,340 --> 00:00:50,790
write,

19
00:00:50,840 --> 00:00:51,610
and read them.

20
00:00:52,440 --> 00:00:54,850
Attention Residuals
store each update in its

21
00:00:54,850 --> 00:00:57,190
own slot and read them with one softmax.

22
00:00:57,660 --> 00:00:58,350
Unrolled,

23
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:00,830
every rule sets the strength of the input,

24
00:01:01,040 --> 00:01:02,520
the total weight of the updates,

25
00:01:02,520 --> 00:01:04,490
and the share of each update.

26
00:01:05,630 --> 00:01:07,600
Loopback sets the three separately.

27
00:01:08,190 --> 00:01:11,040
The input keeps a weight
of one on its own path,

28
00:01:11,150 --> 00:01:14,660
so the stack still returns its
input when the updates vanish.

29
00:01:15,190 --> 00:01:16,760
A decay bounds the total,

30
00:01:17,030 --> 00:01:19,860
and attention shares it
across the stored updates.

31
00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:23,950
Here are our training
curves at all five scales.

32
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:28,590
Loopback's loss is lower from early
in training all the way to the end.

33
00:01:31,050 --> 00:01:34,740
From 191M to 3B parameters,

34
00:01:35,190 --> 00:01:38,363
Loopback reaches lower
loss than the standard

35
00:01:38,363 --> 00:01:42,320
Transformer while using only
40–75% of its parameters.

36
00:01:43,690 --> 00:01:45,620
At all three sizes we tested,

37
00:01:45,810 --> 00:01:50,540
it has the lowest loss and the highest
benchmark average of all nine designs.

38
00:01:53,100 --> 00:01:57,150
We also held the parameter count
fixed and traded width for depth.

39
00:01:57,440 --> 00:01:59,690
As the model gets deeper and narrower,

40
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:02,190
the standard Transformer loses ground,

41
00:02:02,340 --> 00:02:04,870
while Loopback's margin
grows at both scales.

42
00:02:06,140 --> 00:02:07,190
That's Loopback.

43
00:02:07,560 --> 00:02:08,410
Fewer parameters,

44
00:02:08,680 --> 00:02:09,370
lower loss.

45
00:02:09,920 --> 00:02:12,270
The paper and code are
on our project page.

